                <pb facs="es_d9-1" n="1" xml:id="es_d9-1"/>

                <head>ES–9. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On October 1, 1959, the International Monetary Fund set up a $7,500,000 standby
                    credit which the Salvadoran Central Bank could draw on for balance of payments
                    purposes until March 31, 1960. By using this credit the Central Bank could
                    maintain a high level of liquid reserves without selling gold before coffee
                    sales added to reserves at the end of the year. For additional information, see
                    International Monetary Fund, <hi rendition="#italic">Annual Report of the
                        Executive Directors for the Fiscal Year Ended April 30, 1960</hi>
                    (Washington, D.C., 1960), pages 18 and 21–23.</p>
            </div>
